{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghx2XXyLzgsXcu7kwDBC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For [Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺ I get **Dq ≈ 1898 cm⁻¹**, **B ≈ 557 cm⁻¹** and C ≈ 2505 cm⁻¹. These come from the standard first-order strong-field formulas, not from a full diagonalisation of the d⁶ matrices.\n\n**Approach**\n\n[Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺ is a low-spin d⁶ complex (t₂g⁶), so its ground state is ¹A₁g. The two spin-allowed bands come from promoting one electron, t₂g⁵e_g¹. In the strong-field limit their energies are:\n\n- ¹A₁g → ¹T₁g: E₁ = 10Dq − C\n- ¹A₁g → ¹T₂g: E₂ = 10Dq + 16B − C\n\n**Steps**\n\n1. Find B from the gap between the bands:\n   E₂ − E₁ = 16B = 25 381 − 16 474 = 8 907 cm⁻¹, so B = 556.7 cm⁻¹.\n2. Use the given C/B = 4.5:\n   C = 4.5 × 556.7 = 2 505 cm⁻¹.\n3. Find Dq from the first band:\n   10Dq = E₁ + C = 16 474 + 2 505 = 18 979 cm⁻¹, so Dq = 1 898 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Checks and caveats**\n\n- Compared with the free Co³⁺ ion (B about 1100 cm⁻¹), B is reduced to roughly half. This nephelauxetic ratio (β ≈ 0.5) is typical for Co(III).\n- The formulas ignore configuration interaction. Fitting the full Tanabe–Sugano matrices with C/B = 4.5 would shift Dq and B by a few percent.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1898, \"B\": 557, \"C\": 2505, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Low-spin d6 strong-field formulas: 16B = band gap, 10Dq = E(1T1g) + C with C = 4.5B.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":193,"cache_read_input_tokens":1480,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":193,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7c0c0e1d-394d-4ab0-b36b-6cd84c9202a7","uuid":"00eae337-3df6-4803-94fe-d6c8d02b8687","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:15:26.486Z","reque